TARGET = Tutorial1
# CONSTRUCTOR ORDER IS THE REVERSE OF THIS LIST. That is measured, not assumed.
#
# The runtime walks .ctors from its end back to its start, so the object listed LAST here is the one
# whose constructor runs FIRST. Two marker objects, one at each end of this list, each writing a
# sequence-numbered line to trace.txt (see Scripts/InitTrace.h), proved it: with
# Scripts/BootMarkerLast.o first and Scripts/BootTrace.o last, the trace begins
#
#     boot: first constructor entered   fcr31 as handed over=00000e00
#     boot: fcr31 after clear=00000000
#     ...
#     ctors: all global constructors complete
#
# Moving those two lines in this file moves those two groups of lines in the trace. The .ctors contents
# are the same either way -- only the order at run time changes.
#
# This is why Scripts/BootTrace.o is LAST and not first. Its constructor clears the FPU exception
# enables (see Scripts/FpuControl.h) and drops the boot.txt marker, and being last in this list is what
# makes it run before every other constructor the game owns: TextureHelper, SoundManager, the Matrix and
# Vector statics, TextureManager, Particle. Its original position, first in this list, meant it ran
# *after* all of them -- which armed the FPU traps for the whole constructor phase, the one part of
# startup that no in-game log can describe, because Log.txt does not exist until main().
#
# Scripts/LaunchDir.o now sits after it, and therefore runs before it. That is deliberate and it does
# not disturb what the paragraph above is protecting: LaunchDir's constructor only chdirs and writes an
# integer trace line (see Scripts/LaunchDir.c), so it executes no floating point and cannot trip traps
# that have not been armed yet. What it has to beat is not BootTrace but the first FILE the program
# opens -- BootTrace's constructor opens trace.txt, and main() resets Log.txt -- so a chdir that ran
# after those would leave the run's only record outside the game folder, or nowhere. It has to be the
# first thing that runs, and this is the position that makes it so.
#
# The trace reports whether this arrangement is still working. On the line "boot: fcr31 as handed over":
#
#     =00000e00   the clear ran before anything else, and this ordering is doing its job
#     =00000000   something cleared the traps before it, so this ordering has silently stopped working
#
# Scripts/LaunchFile.o and Scripts/LaunchDir.o are the two halves of the launch handoff, and only ONE of
# them is this program's. LaunchDir.o (the reader, and the object that must run first) is last for the
# reason above; LaunchFile.o (the writer) is the launcher's and is linked here only because LaunchDir.c
# shares the device list with it, so that the two halves cannot disagree about where the handoff lives.
# See Scripts/LaunchDir.h.
#
# Scripts/Updater.o and Scripts/StateUpdate.o are no longer in OBJS, and that is the shape of the
# Launcher change rather than a cleanup. The game used to update itself, which forced a second program
# (UPDATER.PBP) and a one-way loadexec handoff, because a running game cannot replace its own
# EBOOT.PBP. Updating is now the Launcher's job: it owns the install, so it can write the game binary
# while nothing is running it. Both sources still exist and are still the single implementation of the
# package protocol -- UpdaterApp compiles Updater.cpp as source, so the two binaries cannot drift.
# What the game KEEPS is Aurora/System/NetworkManager and the Multiplayer entry in StateMenu.cpp,
# which is where networking for the planned multiplayer work lives.
#
# Scripts/BootMarkerLast.o is first for the same reason, inverted: it marks the END of the constructor
# phase, so it is the one that has to run last.
#
# Note also that neither constructor carries a priority. A priority looks like the obvious way to force
# one early, but it does the opposite here: GCC emits a prioritised constructor into .ctors.<n>, and the
# PSPSDK link script places .ctors.* after every plain .ctors entry. Given the reverse walk above, that
# puts a prioritised constructor at the end of the array, which is to say first at run time -- a
# different mechanism than this one, and not one to mix with it.
OBJS = Scripts/BootMarkerLast.o \
